WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.968
Our next guest is
an Emmy-nominated actor

00:00:02.968 --> 00:00:05.038
you know from films such as
"Little Miss Sunshine,"

00:00:05.038 --> 00:00:06.673
"Wildlife,"
and "There Will Be Blood."

00:00:06.673 --> 00:00:09.542
He stars as the Riddler
in "The Batman,"

00:00:09.542 --> 00:00:11.344
which is in theaters
and IMAX now.

00:00:11.344 --> 00:00:12.879
Let's take a look.

00:00:12.879 --> 00:00:14.113 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:14.113 --> 00:00:16.148
[ Television playing ]

00:00:16.148 --> 00:00:18.518
-[ Door opens ]
-Police! Hands up!

00:00:18.518 --> 00:00:21.620 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:25.024
-He said put your goddamn
hands up, you son of a bitch!

00:00:25.024 --> 00:00:34.667 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:44.310 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:46.546
-I just ordered a slice
of pumpkin pie.

00:00:48.247 --> 00:00:51.551
-Please welcome back to
the show Paul Dano, everybody!

00:00:51.551 --> 00:00:53.085
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:53.085 --> 00:01:01.461
♪♪

00:01:01.461 --> 00:01:09.835
♪♪

00:01:09.835 --> 00:01:11.371
Hello! How are you?

00:01:11.371 --> 00:01:13.472
-Yeah. Yeah.
I'm well. I'm well.

00:01:13.472 --> 00:01:15.040
Your producer just told me

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:16.943
that she had a nightmare
about me over the weekend.

00:01:16.943 --> 00:01:18.511
-Look at that.
-So that was nice.

00:01:18.511 --> 00:01:19.912
-Making an impact
in people's --

00:01:19.912 --> 00:01:21.647
And, by the way,
with the news right now,

00:01:21.647 --> 00:01:23.550
if you can be the reason
someone has a nightmare,

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:24.784
congratulations.

00:01:24.784 --> 00:01:27.853
-Ha! Thank you.
-You were here last time

00:01:27.853 --> 00:01:29.655
talking about
a film you directed,

00:01:29.655 --> 00:01:33.058
which was a very beautiful film,
a film about a family,

00:01:33.058 --> 00:01:35.161
and a completely different pace

00:01:35.161 --> 00:01:37.530
than what you did here
in "The Batman."

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:41.167
-Yeah. So, luckily,
that's sort of been,

00:01:41.167 --> 00:01:43.302
like, the theme
of just how I work.

00:01:43.302 --> 00:01:47.073
I was in the edit room doing
a sort of quiet family film,

00:01:47.073 --> 00:01:48.507
and then I got a script

00:01:48.507 --> 00:01:50.142
for this thing called
"Escape at Dannemora,"

00:01:50.142 --> 00:01:53.646
which was, like, prison, sex,
you know, physical.

00:01:53.646 --> 00:01:56.382
And I was like, "Great.
That's what I need now."

00:01:56.382 --> 00:01:58.852
Right? And so then I was
on camera for seven months

00:01:58.852 --> 00:02:01.621
in a jail cell
with Benicio Del Toro.

00:02:01.621 --> 00:02:03.889
And after seven months
of being on camera, I was like,

00:02:03.889 --> 00:02:05.358
"I can't act again.
I can't act again."

00:02:05.358 --> 00:02:08.160
And this play called "True West"
came, which I did on Broadway,

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:11.497
and that sort of reinvigorated,
you know, whatever I needed.

00:02:11.497 --> 00:02:13.733
And then after doing a play,
which was not for profit,

00:02:13.733 --> 00:02:16.168
"The Batman" came,
and I was like --

00:02:16.168 --> 00:02:19.371
You know, and so it's just
sort of follow the energy.

00:02:19.371 --> 00:02:21.574
And I play, you know,

00:02:21.574 --> 00:02:23.443
an interesting character
in "The Batman."

00:02:23.443 --> 00:02:24.810
And the next film I have
coming out in the fall

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:26.646
is Steven Spielberg's new film,

00:02:26.646 --> 00:02:28.647
and I play, like,
a beautiful, kind man,

00:02:28.647 --> 00:02:31.350
and I'm just so thankful
after...

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:32.751
after doing the Riddler,

00:02:32.751 --> 00:02:35.688
that that was then
the next thing that came my way.

00:02:35.688 --> 00:02:37.523
So we'll see.
-You know, you talked --

00:02:37.523 --> 00:02:38.991
This is a deeply dark character.

00:02:38.991 --> 00:02:41.727
And even by the standards
of the Riddler,

00:02:41.727 --> 00:02:44.297
this is a very dark take.

00:02:44.297 --> 00:02:46.232
Your wife, Zoe, is obviously
in show business, as well.

00:02:46.232 --> 00:02:48.067
She must understand
what you have to do as an actor.

00:02:48.067 --> 00:02:49.902
With that said, is it easy
for you to leave a job

00:02:49.902 --> 00:02:51.570
like that at home
when you come home?

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:56.276
-Uh, I mean, I'm so scared
to talk about it in public

00:02:56.276 --> 00:02:57.943
because, like,
the Internet, like, they --

00:02:57.943 --> 00:03:00.146
People, like, pick up
on what you say and it's --

00:03:00.146 --> 00:03:01.814
It can kind of get
blown out of proportion.

00:03:01.814 --> 00:03:04.884
But I don't know if you had
the experience doing "SNL,"

00:03:04.884 --> 00:03:06.686
like, where you've got
all this energy

00:03:06.686 --> 00:03:07.953
going toward Saturday night.

00:03:07.953 --> 00:03:09.922
-Yeah.
-And the stakes are high.

00:03:09.922 --> 00:03:11.357
And it's adrenaline.

00:03:11.357 --> 00:03:13.192
And it's -- You know,
and then after the show,

00:03:13.192 --> 00:03:14.994
you can't just,
like, go to bed, right?

00:03:14.994 --> 00:03:16.295
-Yeah, we rock out.

00:03:16.295 --> 00:03:17.963
-I mean, in your case,
you guys get to go rock out.

00:03:17.963 --> 00:03:20.332
Now, I didn't rock out
after a long day of shooting,

00:03:20.332 --> 00:03:23.002
but it takes a while
to come down.

00:03:23.002 --> 00:03:25.938
-Yes.
-Right? So that's all.

00:03:25.938 --> 00:03:27.406
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:03:27.406 --> 00:03:29.241
-That's a very good way
of putting it.

00:03:29.241 --> 00:03:31.344
Now, at least you,
I would imagine,

00:03:31.344 --> 00:03:33.712
leave your wardrobe at work.

00:03:33.712 --> 00:03:38.784
Because this was a terrifying
take on the Riddler,

00:03:38.784 --> 00:03:42.655
which included wrapping
your head in Saran wrap.

00:03:42.655 --> 00:03:44.990
-Yeah, so,
I've been asked, you know,

00:03:44.990 --> 00:03:47.927
what did you take from the set
to remember this job by?

00:03:47.927 --> 00:03:49.662
And I'm like,
"Nothing. Nothing."

00:03:49.662 --> 00:03:51.397
-Well, Saran wrap,
you could take

00:03:51.397 --> 00:03:52.865
because it's just helpful
in the kitchen.

00:03:52.865 --> 00:03:55.067
-Well, you know, they actually
don't want me talking about

00:03:55.067 --> 00:03:56.769
Saran wrap, Seth.
-Oh, interesting.

00:03:56.769 --> 00:03:58.404
-I think I have to say
cling wrap.

00:03:58.404 --> 00:04:00.072
They don't want
to be associated --

00:04:00.072 --> 00:04:01.307
-Well, the problem is,

00:04:01.307 --> 00:04:04.610
I have a deal with
Saran wrap, personally.

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:07.380
-Oh, you do? Well, this is
a real conflict of --

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:10.783
-It's a very awkward moment.
-They might drop ya.

00:04:10.783 --> 00:04:12.251
They might drop ya
after this one.

00:04:12.251 --> 00:04:15.654
-I'm sorry I keep saying it.
This is very bad.

00:04:15.654 --> 00:04:18.624
Oh, boy, oh, boy.

00:04:18.624 --> 00:04:20.459
But it was --
One of the things I read

00:04:20.459 --> 00:04:23.462
is that this version of
the Riddler is a serial killer

00:04:23.462 --> 00:04:25.798
and that the Zodiac Killer
was actually one of the --

00:04:25.798 --> 00:04:28.000
Is this something you came up
with, with the director?

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:29.602
-Yeah, so,
that's something Matt Reeves,

00:04:29.602 --> 00:04:31.437
who's just wonderful
and I loved working with him,

00:04:31.437 --> 00:04:32.972
cited in the beginning.

00:04:32.972 --> 00:04:35.241
So when you get the phone call,
the Riddler, Batman, it's like,

00:04:35.241 --> 00:04:36.542
"Oh, my God.
That's so exciting."

00:04:36.542 --> 00:04:39.978
And then when he says
"Zodiac Killer," you go, "Okay."

00:04:39.978 --> 00:04:42.048
You know, it's like,
okay, that's, like,

00:04:42.048 --> 00:04:45.985
a much more sort of real...
-Right.

00:04:45.985 --> 00:04:47.586
That's not question marks
on a green suit.

00:04:47.586 --> 00:04:48.888
-Yes, from the comic book.

00:04:48.888 --> 00:04:51.824
So it actually was
the kind of thing where --

00:04:51.824 --> 00:04:54.226
especially now
that I'm a parent, like,

00:04:54.226 --> 00:04:55.762
a lot of my research for this

00:04:55.762 --> 00:04:58.197
I did during the daytime
at coffee shops

00:04:58.197 --> 00:05:01.034
because I just didn't want those
books, like, next to my bed.

00:05:01.034 --> 00:05:02.368
-Yeah.
-Do you know?

00:05:02.368 --> 00:05:04.136
So it was --
-You also don't want them

00:05:04.136 --> 00:05:06.105
next to the bed
because your kids are like,

00:05:06.105 --> 00:05:07.473
"What are these serial-killer
books for?"

00:05:07.473 --> 00:05:08.875
And you say, "Research."

00:05:08.875 --> 00:05:11.643
[ Laughter ]

00:05:11.643 --> 00:05:13.846
-Yes. Yes.

00:05:13.846 --> 00:05:16.315
-"Daddy has a job to do."
-Yeah.

00:05:16.315 --> 00:05:18.818
[ Laughter ]
Oh, God.

00:05:18.818 --> 00:05:20.653
Someday I'll be faced with that.

00:05:20.653 --> 00:05:22.421
-"It's just hard
'cause it's so political

00:05:22.421 --> 00:05:25.390
breaking into it as a job.

00:05:25.390 --> 00:05:27.293
You gotta know somebody."

00:05:27.293 --> 00:05:29.395
[ Laughter ]

00:05:29.395 --> 00:05:31.164
But you also are
a reader of comics.

00:05:31.164 --> 00:05:33.899
So it must be cool to --
You know,

00:05:33.899 --> 00:05:36.602
I grew up reading comic books
and still do,

00:05:36.602 --> 00:05:38.804
and I just imagine being a part
of something like this,

00:05:38.804 --> 00:05:40.673
which is being taken
so seriously

00:05:40.673 --> 00:05:42.341
and treated like art,
must be a blast.

00:05:42.341 --> 00:05:44.042
-It's the best bit of homework.

00:05:44.042 --> 00:05:47.180
And my character
has a particular fascination

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:48.381
with The Batman in the film.

00:05:48.381 --> 00:05:50.983
And so I just devoured
Batman comics

00:05:50.983 --> 00:05:52.384
the whole time we were shooting.

00:05:52.384 --> 00:05:54.487
It was just a way to sort of
keep it in my system.

00:05:54.487 --> 00:05:56.122
But, yeah, going back
to being a kid,

00:05:56.122 --> 00:05:57.356
I used to collect some comics.

00:05:57.356 --> 00:05:59.658
I was --
I was more of an X-Men kid.

00:05:59.658 --> 00:06:01.494
-Okay. That's --
We appreciate your honesty.

00:06:01.494 --> 00:06:04.030
-But now I'm, like,
Batman superfan.

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:06.232
I mean -- And, really, I am.
I just -- I love it.

00:06:06.232 --> 00:06:08.367
-I heard you name-drop
"Court of Owls"

00:06:08.367 --> 00:06:10.002
in an interview,
which is a great book.

00:06:10.002 --> 00:06:11.370
-Dude, that is
one of my favorites.

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:13.005
-That's when I knew
you were the real deal.

00:06:13.005 --> 00:06:14.340
I was like, "Look at you.
Alright, yeah."

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:17.042
-Yeah, that one's really good.

00:06:17.042 --> 00:06:18.944
It's just --
I mean, I have to say

00:06:18.944 --> 00:06:21.079
that being
a part of that culture,

00:06:21.079 --> 00:06:23.515
it's not something
I anticipated liking.

00:06:23.515 --> 00:06:25.451
I feel like
I'm kind of a shy person.

00:06:25.451 --> 00:06:27.753
But I love the Batman fans

00:06:27.753 --> 00:06:29.555
and I've loved sharing
this film with them.

00:06:29.555 --> 00:06:31.557
So -- Including
the comic-book world.

00:06:31.557 --> 00:06:33.792
So that's been really fun.

00:06:33.792 --> 00:06:35.294
-I want to ask --
you know, obviously,

00:06:35.294 --> 00:06:37.029
you've got Robert Pattinson
walking around in the suit.

00:06:37.029 --> 00:06:39.732
Colin Farrell, you know --
much has been made

00:06:39.732 --> 00:06:42.135
of his transformation
as the Penguin.

00:06:42.135 --> 00:06:44.236
Did you ever --
Was it ever jarring

00:06:44.236 --> 00:06:46.505
when you saw your co-stars
on set?

00:06:46.505 --> 00:06:48.007
-Well, I was jealous of Colin

00:06:48.007 --> 00:06:50.342
because the makeup
was so incredible.

00:06:50.342 --> 00:06:51.911
And I was like,
"That's not fair."

00:06:51.911 --> 00:06:53.312
-Yeah.
-You know?

00:06:53.312 --> 00:06:54.881
-Because you had to wear
cling wrap.

00:06:54.881 --> 00:06:57.516
-Yeah. Nice.

00:06:57.516 --> 00:06:59.919
-Yeah. I think
we're back in business.

00:06:59.919 --> 00:07:02.554
-And the clip that we showed

00:07:02.554 --> 00:07:04.890
was one of my favorite nights
of filming

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:10.062
just because seeing The Batman
out the window, I just...

00:07:10.062 --> 00:07:11.297
This is, like, a moment.

00:07:11.297 --> 00:07:13.265
Like, you know --
And for me it was, too.

00:07:13.265 --> 00:07:15.000
For the character, it was.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:17.803
Being on our backlot,
which they built, you know,

00:07:17.803 --> 00:07:20.038
this L train,
an elevated train,

00:07:20.038 --> 00:07:22.941
with the streets of Gotham,
seeing Rob,

00:07:22.941 --> 00:07:25.911
seeing Colin,
seeing Zoe Kravitz,

00:07:25.911 --> 00:07:28.780
I mean, it was really --

00:07:28.780 --> 00:07:30.750
It's definitely, like,

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:32.584
one of the coolest experiences
I've had.

00:07:32.584 --> 00:07:34.453
-Well, I will say personally,
being a fan of yours,

00:07:34.453 --> 00:07:37.256
when you hear a cast like this
and a director like this,

00:07:37.256 --> 00:07:39.258
it's very exciting
as a viewer to say,

00:07:39.258 --> 00:07:40.425
"Oh, these are
the kind of people

00:07:40.425 --> 00:07:41.827
that are gonna take
good care of this."

00:07:41.827 --> 00:07:43.496
And you guys really did.
Congratulations.

00:07:43.496 --> 00:07:45.564
Thanks so much for being here.
It's always a pleasure.

00:07:45.564 --> 00:07:47.400
-Thank you. Yeah. Yeah.
-Paul Dano, everybody.

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:49.402
"The Batman" is in theaters
and IMAX now.

